title: Proposition 35
---
order: 35
---
short_label: III.35
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If in a circle two straight lines cut one another, then the rectangle contained by the segments of the one equals the rectangle contained by the segments of the other.
----
proof:

For in the circle {ABCD} let the two straight lines {AC} and {BD} cut one another at the point {E}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="260" height="220" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.35a diagram" src="propIII35a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The first of Joyce's two cases: both chords through the center, so E
    // is the center and the four segments are radii. Joyce's figure draws
    // AC and BD whole; the proof names their four segments, so those are
    // added as fully transparent lines (;0;0;0) — the static figure is
    // unchanged, the names exist only so a slide can light them. No
    // rectangles are drawn (they would not fit), so "the rectangle AE by
    // EC" keeps its refs literal, as in II.1.
    //
    // Every draggable here (A, C, B — checked against the bundle) is a
    // given that slide 1 introduces, so nothing is deferred. E (midpoint)
    // and D (extend) are derived and so are listed explicitly.
    var GIVEN = ["A","C","E","ABCD","B","D","AC","BD"];
    var SEGS  = GIVEN.concat(["AE","EC","DE","EB"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.35a",
        pivot: "E",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;60,50",
            "C;point;free;200,170",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,C;black;green",
            "ABCD;circle;radius;E,A;0;0;black;random",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABCD,50,110",
            "D;point;extend;B,E,B,E",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — AC and BD already draw them.
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            "EC;line;connect;E,C;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "EB;line;connect;E,B;0;0;0"
        ],
        aliases: { "CA": "AC", "DB": "BD", "EA": "AE", "CE": "EC",
                   "ED": "DE", "BE": "EB" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”), split at its seams.
            { text: "“If in {a circle|ABCD} two straight lines cut one another,”",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AC","BD","E"],
              // Draw the two lines, then carry B round the circle and back:
              // its chord turns with it (D is B through E), so "two straight
              // lines" reads as any pair, not this one.
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "AC", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "BD", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "B", name: geomlib.A.Point.followPath, args: { arc: { center: "E", sweep: 50 } } } ] } },
            { text: "“then the rectangle contained by the segments of the one”",
              visible: SEGS,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC"] },
            { text: "“equals the rectangle contained by the segments of the other.”",
              visible: SEGS,
              highlighted: ["DE","EB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.35" }] },
            { text: "For in the circle {ABCD} let the two straight lines {AC} and {BD} cut one another at the point {E}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AC","BD","E"] },
            { text: "I say that the rectangle {AE} by {EC} equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB}.",
              visible: SEGS,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","DE","EB"] },
            { text: "If now {AC} and {BD} are through the center, so that {E} is the center of the circle {ABCD},",
              visible: SEGS,
              highlighted: ["AC","BD","E"] },
            { text: "it is manifest that, {AE}, {EC}, {DE}, and {EB} being equal, the rectangle {AE} by {EC} also equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB}.",
              visible: SEGS,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","DE","EB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "AE, EC, DE and EB are radii of the same circle", ref: "I.Def.15" }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.35") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "I.Def.15": "/elements/books/bookI/definitions/defI15/",
        "I.Post.1": "/elements/books/bookI/postulates/post1/",
        "I.12":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI12/",
        "I.47":     "/elements/books/bookI/propositions/propI47/",
        "II.5":     "/elements/books/bookII/propositions/propII5/",
        "III.1":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII1/",
        "III.3":    "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII3/",
        "C.N.1":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn1/",
        "C.N.2":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn2/",
        "C.N.3":    "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn3/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that the rectangle  {AE} by {EC} equals the rectangle  {DE} by {EB}.

If now {AC} and {BD} are through the center, so that {E} is the center of the circle {ABCD}, it is manifest that, {AE}, {EC}, {DE}, and {EB} being equal, the rectangle {AE} by {EC} also equals the rectangle  {DE} by {EB}.

<hr width="30%">

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="260" height="270" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.35b diagram" src="propIII35b.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The general case, and the whole of the argument: a perpendicular from
    // the center bisects each chord (III.3), II.5 turns the rectangle into a
    // difference of squares, and I.47 moves that difference up to the
    // radius. Joyce's figure draws the perpendiculars and the three joins;
    // the proof names the segments of the two chords (AG, GC, AE, EC, EG,
    // DE, EB), which are added here as fully transparent lines (;0;0;0).
    // No squares are drawn, so "the square on FE" keeps its refs literal.
    //
    // Two right-angle markers, at the feet G and H. Markers are hidden in
    // the static figure (showAngles defaults false), so Joyce's arc-free
    // diagram is untouched. In his figure E lies on the C side of G and on
    // the B side of H, so the one marker at G serves both I.47 triangles
    // (FGE and FGC share the ray GC) and likewise at H.
    //
    // Draggables, checked against the bundle: A, C, B, D and F. The first
    // four are the given chords, introduced on slide 1. F is the center,
    // which the proof does not take until its second sentence, so it is
    // deferred out of the every-slide auto-union. G, H (midpoint) and E
    // (intersection) are derived and listed per slide. GF' is the
    // colourless carrier of F and is kept out of every set.
    var GIVEN = ["A","C","AC","ABCD","B","D","BD","E"];
    var CTR   = GIVEN.concat(["F"]);
    var PERP  = CTR.concat(["G","FG","H","HF"]);
    var JOIN  = PERP.concat(["BF","CF","EF"]);
    var RT    = JOIN.concat(["angFGC"]);
    var BIS   = RT.concat(["AG","GC"]);
    var CUT   = BIS.concat(["AE","EC","EG"]);
    var SAME  = CUT.concat(["DE","EB","angFHB"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.35b",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,80",
            "C;point;free;160,230",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C",
            "G;point;midpoint;A,C",
            "GF';line;perpendicular;G,A;0;0;0",
            "F;point;lineSlider;GF',130,140",
            "FG;line;connect;F,G",
            "ABCD;circle;radius;F,A;0;0;black;random",
            "B;point;circleSlider;ABCD,100,230",
            "D;point;circleSlider;ABCD,210,80",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D",
            "E;point;intersection;A,C,B,D",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            "BF;line;connect;B,F",
            "CF;line;connect;C,F",
            "H;point;midpoint;B,D",
            "HF;line;connect;H,F",
            // Hover / highlight targets only — AC and BD already draw them.
            "AG;line;connect;A,G;0;0;0",
            "GC;line;connect;G,C;0;0;0",
            "AE;line;connect;A,E;0;0;0",
            "EC;line;connect;E,C;0;0;0",
            "EG;line;connect;E,G;0;0;0",
            "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
            "EB;line;connect;E,B;0;0;0",
            // The right angles at the feet of the two perpendiculars.
            "angFGC;sector;angleMarker;G,F,C;0;0;red;red",
            "angFHB;sector;angleMarker;H,F,B;0;0;red;red"
        ],
        deferDraggables: ["F"],
        aliases: { "CA": "AC", "DB": "BD", "GF": "FG", "FH": "HF",
                   "FB": "BF", "FC": "CF", "FE": "EF", "GA": "AG",
                   "CG": "GC", "EA": "AE", "CE": "EC", "GE": "EG",
                   "ED": "DE", "BE": "EB",
                   "FGC": "angFGC", "CGF": "angFGC", "EGF": "angFGC",
                   "FGE": "angFGC", "FHB": "angFHB", "BHF": "angFHB" },
        slides: [
            { text: "Next let {AC} and {DB} not be through the center.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["AC","BD"] },
            // Joyce's "Take the center F let the center of ABCD" is
            // reproduced as it stands on his page.
            { text: "Take the center {F} let the center of {ABCD}.",
              visible: CTR,
              highlighted: ["F"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "F", name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "the center of the circle", ref: "III.1" }] },
            { text: "Draw {FG} and {FH} from {F} perpendicular to the straight lines {AC} and {DB}.",
              visible: PERP,
              highlighted: ["AC", "BD", "FG", "G", "HF", "H"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "FG", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "G",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "HF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "H",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear } ] },
              justifications: [{ claim: "perpendiculars from F to AC and DB", ref: "I.12" }] },
            { text: "Join {FB}, {FC}, and {FE}.",
              visible: JOIN,
              highlighted: [],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "BF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "CF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect },
                  { elem: "EF", name: geomlib.A.Line.straightEdgeConnect } ] },
              justifications: [{ ref: "I.Post.1" }] },
            // His III.3 sentence is one clause of premise and one of
            // conclusion; two slides, so the bisection lands on its own.
            { text: "Then, since a straight line {GF} through the center cuts a straight line {AC} not through the center at right angles, it also bisects it,",
              visible: RT,
              highlighted: ["FG", "AC", "angFGC"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angFGC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] } },
            { text: "therefore {AG} equals {GC}.",
              visible: BIS,
              highlighted: ["AG","GC","G"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "a perpendicular through the center bisects the chord", ref: "III.3" }] },
            { text: "Since, then, the straight line {AC} has been cut into equal parts at {G} and into unequal parts at {E}, the rectangle {AE} by {EC} together with the square on {EG} equals the square on {GC}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["AC","G","E"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "equal segments at G, unequal at E", ref: "II.5" }] },
            { text: "Add the square on {GF}. Therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the sum of the squares on {GE} and {GF} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GF}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["FG","EG","GC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "add the square on GF to each", ref: "C.N.2" }] },
            { text: "But the square on {FE} equals the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GF}, and the square on {FC} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GF}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["EF","EG","FG","CF","GC","angFGC"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "the right angle at G in the triangles FGE and FGC", ref: "I.47" }] },
            { text: "Therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} equals the square on {FC}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","EF","CF"] },
            { text: "And {FC} equals {FB}, therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {EF} equals the square on {FB}.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["CF","BF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "FC and FB are radii of the same circle", ref: "I.Def.15" }] },
            { text: "For the same reason, also, the rectangle {DE} by {EB} plus the square on {FE} equals the square on {FB}.",
              visible: SAME,
              highlighted: ["HF", "DE", "EB", "EF", "BF", "angFHB"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "angFHB", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "FH through the center bisects DB at H", ref: "III.3" },
                  { claim: "equal segments at H, unequal at E",     ref: "II.5" },
                  { claim: "the right angle at H",                  ref: "I.47" } ] },
            { text: "But the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} was also proved equal to the square on {FB}, therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB} plus the square on {FE}.",
              visible: SAME,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","DE","EB","EF"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "each equals the square on FB", ref: "C.N.1" }] },
            { text: "Subtract the square on {FE} from each. Therefore the remaining rectangle {AE} by {EC} equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB}.",
              visible: SAME,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","DE","EB"],
              justifications: [{ claim: "subtract the square on FE from each", ref: "C.N.3" }] },
            { text: "Therefore if in a circle two straight lines cut one another, then the rectangle contained by the segments of the one equals the rectangle contained by the segments of the other.",
              visible: SAME,
              highlighted: ["AE","EC","DE","EB"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });
</script>
</figure>

Next let {AC} and {DB} not be through the center.  Take the center {F} let the center of {ABCD}.  Draw {FG} and {FH} from {F} perpendicular  to the straight lines {AC} and {DB}.  Join {FB}, {FC}, and {FE}. [!just III.1; I.12]

Then, since a straight line {GF} through the center cuts a straight line {AC} not through the center at right angles, it also bisects it, therefore {AG} equals {GC}. [!just III.3]

Since, then, the straight line {AC} has been cut into equal parts at {G} and into unequal parts at {E}, the rectangle {AE} by {EC} together with the square on {EG} equals the square on {GC}. [!just II.5]

Add the square on {GF}.  Therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the sum of the squares on {GE} and {GF} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GF}.

But the square on {FE} equals the sum of the squares on {EG} and {GF}, and the square on {FC} equals the sum of the squares on {CG} and {GF}. Therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} equals the square on {FC}. [!just I.47]

And {FC} equals {FB}, therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {EF} equals the square on {FB}.

For the same reason, also, the rectangle {DE} by {EB} plus the square on {FE} equals the square on {FB}.

But the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} was also proved equal to the square on {FB}, therefore the rectangle {AE} by {EC} plus the square on {FE} equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB} plus the square on {FE}.

Subtract the square on {FE} from each.  Therefore the remaining rectangle {AE} by {EC} equals the rectangle {DE} by {EB}.

Therefore *if in a circle two straight lines cut one another, then the rectangle contained by the segments of the one equals the rectangle contained by the segments of the other.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

By means of proposition @VI.16, the statement, "the rectangle {AE:canvas_0,canvas_1} by {EC:canvas_0,canvas_1} equals the rectangle {DE:canvas_0,canvas_1} by {EB:canvas_0,canvas_1}," may be converted into one about ratios, namely, "{AE:canvas_0,canvas_1} : {EB:canvas_0,canvas_1} = {DE:canvas_0,canvas_1} : {EC:canvas_0,canvas_1}."

This proposition is not used in the rest of the *Elements.*
